<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- Dark is the product (§12): there is no light variant to fall back to.
         The window paints the unlit ground so the very first frame, before
         Compose draws, is already Ribbon's ground and not a white flash. -->
    <style name="Theme.Ribbon" parent="@android:style/Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/unlit</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:enforceNavigationBarContrast">false</item>
        <item name="android:enforceStatusBarContrast">false</item>
    </style>

    <!-- The launch window.

         §05 says there is no splash screen, and that rule is kept in the
         sense that matters: nothing is *inserted*. Android 12 and later show
         a system splash on every cold start whether an app asks for one or
         not — it cannot be declined, only styled — so the choice was never
         "splash or no splash", it was "our mark or the launcher icon on a
         grey plate". Owner's call (deviation A28): it is the Wave, on the
         unlit ground, unfurling.

         What is deliberately absent: no wordmark, no tagline, no version, no
         "welcome back". The mark and the ground, and then the room. -->
    <style name="Theme.Ribbon.Splash" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <!-- **The `android:` prefix is the whole thing.** These were the
             androidx compat attributes and the platform never read them: on
             API 31 and later the *platform* draws the splash from its own
             `android:windowSplashScreen*` attributes, and the compat ones
             only do anything below 31. minSdk is 33, so every device on
             earth was falling through to the system default — the launcher
             icon on a plate.

             Because minSdk is already past 31 these live here rather than in
             a values-v31 folder; there is no device that would read a
             pre-31 variant. -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/unlit</item>
        <!-- **The ground, and the mark is the app's.**

             This was `@drawable/splash_wave_animated`, and on the owner's
             Pixel 9 Pro XL it did not animate: the mark appeared whole and
             then faded to the room. That drawable was checked first —
             inflated, started, drawn frame by frame — and it was correct, so
             what could not be established is why the platform declined to
             play it. The splash is drawn by the system, from this theme, in
             another process, before the app exists: there is nothing here to
             see, test or fix. The drawable and its two animators are deleted
             rather than left unreferenced.

             So the launch window carries the ground alone and
             `design/LaunchMark.kt` draws the mark on the app's first frame,
             where the unfurl runs on the same clock as everything else in
             the product and under the same reduce-motion token (§11).

             An empty vector rather than no value at all, because naming
             nothing hands the slot back to the launcher icon on a plate. -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/splash_ground</item>
        <!-- Nothing in the launch window animates now, so there is no
             duration to declare. Left at zero rather than removed: the
             platform reads this attribute whether or not it is set, and a
             value that says "no animation" is clearer than a default. -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration">0</item>
        <!-- No icon background: the mark is a shape on a ground, and a
             circular plate behind it would be an app icon rather than a mark.
             Without one the icon is not masked.

             `postSplashScreenTheme` stays a compat attribute — it is the
             library's own, read by `installSplashScreen()` rather than by the
             platform, and it is what swaps the window over to Theme.Ribbon
             once the splash is done. -->
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.Ribbon</item>
    </style>
</resources>
